Hi guys,

Just an FYI that I was able to get the VNC stuff working on my Mavericks
home Mac using the same steps for my work Mac.
I didn't have to right click that "TurboVNC.pkg" file, however;
I was able to just open and run it with a double-click.

I played around with NV last night and have some comments/questions for Wei. 

First, though, I have to say I was impressed by the tool when looked at a 1.0 degree grid.
It was quick and I didn't manage to make it core dump.
I only briefly tried the 0.25 degree grid. This one was noticeably slower.
I would have expected maybe 4x slow down, but it seemed a little more than that. Maybe I was just tired.

Here are my comments:

"nv4se1.0d" is awkward to type. Also, the "1" can look like an "l" in documentation.
I suggest making this just "nv" if possible, as we discussed last night,
or something easier like "nv4camse".

The default globe that comes up is too big for the screen.
It would be nice to have it fully visible upon first opening it.

There were a lot of options on the plotting window (like "help") that either don't work,
or I have no clue what they do.
I think for the purpose of making this tool easy,
you don't want a bunch of options or features that don't really work or do anything.

For every variable I plotted, I kept wishing I had more information (metadata) about it.
I think the dimension sizes came up under "Variable Info",
but this text started overlapping and was hard to read.
It would be good to have at least the "long_name" and "units".
Really, all the metadata should be accessible,
because you don't always know what "TMQ" means.

What is the "reset" button supposed to do?
I thought it would get me back to the initial state, but it didn't seem to work.

When you click on the "flat" option, I think it should give you the map right side up.
:-)  I kept getting it upside down and had to reorient it. 

The map outlines seem to be really irregular.
Sometimes they are lines, sometimes they have gaps,
and sometimes they become dots.
This is a bit disconcerting.
I think the "dots" thing happened when using the slider with a 3D variable.
It would be nice to have a "map off" button like you do with the color bar.
Maybe there is one and I didn't see it.

It wasn't obvious to me how to quit out of "nv".
I found a "quit" button, but then the graphics window stayed up.
I had to "x" out of this.

Just to see what would happen,
I tried opening a 0.25 deg file with the 1.0 "nv" script.
It didn't complain, but it brought up a plot that looked wrong.

The text for the 2D and 3D variables seemed small.
(And I agree with Steve that there should be labels indicating that these are 2D and 3D variables).
Can the text size be increased through NV?
All-in-all, I was very encouraged by what I saw. Once I had a real mouse,
it was much more intuitive to zoom in on the image and rotate it.
I always hate using the laptop mouse pad for these things.

I will try to test some more today or this weekend.
I hope to install the VNC software on my windows machine at home.

